Z||000002||Oreif||07-06-2000||11:02 PM||||What did you use to paint the logo on the center of the steering wheel? I'd like to do mine but haven't found anything flexible enough.||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||07-07-2000||02:03 AM||||Cool DJ, I actually just did the same thing on my Formula's steering wheel. I painted the center piece as well as the two small pieces on the int. door handles. I have tan int., so I painted them all a tan color that matches perfectly! It was just basic Testors hobby paint.<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||DJRice||07-07-2000||08:38 AM||||I used an acrylic paint, after two days it still hasnt set right....At least I can clean it up with water.<P>I think Ill give the testors paint a try.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||87ECO5||07-07-2000||09:49 AM||||You'll want paint that's flexible.  From experience, the Testor's model paint will crack and flake.<P>An idea I had was to buy a small can of the Dupli-color vinyl paint, spray it into a bottle, and use a brush to apply it.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||07-07-2000||11:36 AM||||I would probably covered the opening of the scoop instead of the opening of the panel. That speaker cloth will probably rot aand get sucked in. But what do I know I didnt put anything on my scoop. Thats what the air filter is for. keeping out the big stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||07-08-2000||12:11 PM||||Actually, thats metal so its not going anywhere.  The only reason I did that wast the outer rim of the air duct was faded and was very obvious.  ||||reg||1
